    Object.assign(meta("hard"), {
      id: "ml-98",
      short: "Cross-attention lets the decoder query the encoder output. Queries come from the decoder's current hidden states while keys and values come from the encoder's final representations, so each decoding step computes attention weights over source positions and takes a weighted sum of encoder information. This is how translation, summarisation and speech models align generated tokens with the input.",
      detail: L(
        "#### Encoder-decoder Transformer",
        "The original Transformer (Vaswani et al., 2017) has two stacks:",
        "- **Encoder**: N layers of self-attention plus feed-forward networks that turn the source sequence (for example a French sentence) into contextual representations `H_enc` (length S × d).",
        "- **Decoder**: N layers, each with three sublayers:",
        "  1. **Masked (causal) self-attention** over the target tokens generated so far, so position t cannot see future tokens.",
        "  2. **Cross-attention (encoder-decoder attention)** over the encoder output.",
        "  3. Position-wise feed-forward network.",
        "  Each sublayer has residual connections and layer normalisation.",
        "",
        "#### Cross-attention mechanics",
        "In scaled dot-product attention `Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QKᵀ / √d_k) V`, the sources of Q, K, V differ:",
        "- **Queries Q** = `X_dec W_Q` computed from the **decoder's current hidden states** (the output of its masked self-attention sublayer): \"what information do I need now?\"",
        "- **Keys K** = `H_enc W_K` and **Values V** = `H_enc W_V` computed from the **encoder output**: \"what information is available at each source position?\".",
        "The result for each decoder position is a **weighted combination of source representations**, with weights showing which source tokens are relevant for generating the current target token (soft alignment, interpretable as attention maps in translation: \"chat\" attends to \"cat\"). There is **no causal mask** because the entire source is available; only padding masks are applied.",
        "```python",
        "import torch, torch.nn.functional as F",
        "def cross_attention(dec, enc, Wq, Wk, Wv, heads, src_pad_mask=None):",
        "    B, T, d = dec.shape; S = enc.shape[1]; dh = d // heads",
        "    q = (dec @ Wq).view(B, T, heads, dh).transpose(1, 2)        # from decoder",
        "    k = (enc @ Wk).view(B, S, heads, dh).transpose(1, 2)        # from encoder",
        "    v = (enc @ Wv).view(B, S, heads, dh).transpose(1, 2)",
        "    scores = q @ k.transpose(-2, -1) / dh ** 0.5                # (B, heads, T, S)",
        "    if src_pad_mask is not None: scores = scores.masked_fill(src_pad_mask[:, None, None, :], float(\"-inf\"))",
        "    return (F.softmax(scores, -1) @ v).transpose(1, 2).reshape(B, T, d)",
        "```",
        "Multi-head cross-attention runs several such heads in parallel to capture different alignment relations, followed by an output projection.",
        "",
        "#### Inference efficiency",
        "During autoregressive generation, `H_enc` does not change, so **K and V for cross-attention are computed once per layer and cached**, while self-attention KV cache grows each step. Cross-attention cost per step is O(S·d) per layer.",
        "",
        "#### How it differs from self-attention",
        "| | Decoder self-attention | Cross-attention |",
        "| --- | --- | --- |",
        "| Q from | decoder states | decoder states |",
        "| K, V from | decoder states (previous tokens) | encoder outputs |",
        "| Mask | causal | padding only |",
        "| Purpose | model target-side context | condition on the source |",
        "",
        "#### Applications",
        "- **Machine translation, summarisation, T5/BART/mT5**, text-to-text tasks.",
        "- **Speech recognition and translation** (Whisper: audio encoder, text decoder with cross-attention).",
        "- **Image captioning** (image encoder, text decoder), visual question answering, **Flamingo** (gated cross-attention layers inserted into a frozen LLM to attend to visual features), BLIP-2 Q-Former.",
        "- **Diffusion models**: Stable Diffusion's U-Net uses cross-attention where image latents (queries) attend to text-encoder embeddings (keys and values), conditioning generation on the prompt; attention maps enable prompt-to-prompt editing.",
        "- **Perceiver and DETR** (object queries attend to image features), retrieval-augmented decoders (RETRO attends to retrieved chunks), multimodal fusion.",
        "- Decoder-only LLMs omit cross-attention and instead concatenate source and target in one sequence with self-attention (prefix), which is simpler but spends decoder capacity on the source.",
        "",
        "#### Pitfalls",
        "Applying a causal mask to cross-attention, forgetting encoder padding masks, recomputing encoder K and V every step, and confusing which side supplies queries versus keys and values."
      ),
      follow: ["How does cross-attention differ from masked self-attention in the decoder?","How are encoder keys and values cached during generation?","Where is cross-attention used beyond text, such as Whisper and Stable Diffusion?"],
      tags: ["transformers","cross-attention","encoder-decoder","attention"]
    }),
    Object.assign(meta("hard"), {
      id: "ml-99",
      short: "Speculative decoding speeds up generation without changing the output distribution. A small draft model quickly proposes several tokens, then the large target model verifies all of them in one parallel forward pass, accepting the longest valid prefix by a rejection-sampling rule and sampling one correction token. Because decoding is memory-bound, verifying k tokens costs about the same as generating one.",
      detail: L(
        "#### The bottleneck",
        "Autoregressive decoding generates one token per forward pass of the large model. For each token the GPU must read **all model weights from memory** (hundreds of GB/s needed) while doing relatively little compute: decoding is **memory-bandwidth-bound**, leaving compute units idle. Generating 500 tokens needs 500 sequential passes.",
        "",
        "#### Idea",
        "Use spare compute: a cheap **draft model** M_q (for example a 1B model, or a smaller variant) proposes `γ` tokens autoregressively; the expensive **target model** M_p scores **all γ proposals in a single parallel forward pass** (like processing a prompt), which costs only slightly more than one decoding step. Then accept or reject the proposals.",
        "",
        "#### Algorithm (Leviathan et al.; Chen et al., 2023)",
        "1. Draft model generates `x_1..x_γ` with probabilities `q(x_i | prefix)`.",
        "2. Target model computes `p(· | prefix, x_1..x_{i-1})` for all positions in one pass.",
        "3. For i = 1..γ: accept `x_i` with probability `min(1, p(x_i) / q(x_i))`. If rejected, **resample** a replacement token from the **residual distribution** `norm(max(0, p − q))` and stop; discard later drafts.",
        "4. If all γ are accepted, sample one **bonus token** from the target's distribution at the next position.",
        "Each iteration yields **at least one** token (the correction or bonus) and up to γ + 1 tokens.",
        "**Correctness**: this rejection-sampling scheme guarantees that the emitted tokens follow **exactly the target model's distribution** (lossless), for any sampling temperature (greedy: accept when the target's argmax equals the draft token).",
        "```python",
        "# conceptual loop",
        "while len(out) < max_new:",
        "    draft = draft_model.generate(prefix, n=gamma, return_probs=True)",
        "    p = target_model.forward(prefix + draft.tokens)            # one pass gives distributions for all positions",
        "    n_acc = 0",
        "    for i, (tok, q) in enumerate(zip(draft.tokens, draft.probs)):",
        "        if random() < min(1, p[i][tok] / q[tok]): n_acc += 1",
        "        else: break",
        "    out += draft.tokens[:n_acc]",
        "    out.append(sample(p[n_acc]) if n_acc == gamma else sample(residual(p[n_acc], q_at(n_acc))))",
        "```",
        "",
        "#### Speedup",
        "Let `α` be the average acceptance rate and `γ` the draft length: expected tokens per target pass = `(1 − α^(γ+1)) / (1 − α)`. With α = 0.8 and γ = 5, about 3.7 tokens per target step. Overall speedup also depends on the **draft cost ratio** `c = cost(draft)/cost(target)`; typical real-world gains are **2x to 3x** with no quality loss, higher for predictable text (code, structured output, translation) and lower at high temperatures or with mismatched drafts.",
        "Acceptance rises when the draft is **well aligned** with the target (same family and tokenizer, distilled from the target).",
        "",
        "#### Variants",
        "- **Separate small model**: needs same vocabulary and tokenizer; extra memory and a second model to serve.",
        "- **Self-speculation / extra heads**: **Medusa** adds multiple decoding heads to the target that predict future tokens, verified using tree attention; **EAGLE/EAGLE-2/3** predicts features autoregressively with a lightweight head for higher acceptance; **Lookahead decoding** (Jacobi iteration).",
        "- **Prompt lookup / n-gram speculation**: copy likely continuations from the prompt (useful for summarisation, editing, RAG), no model needed.",
        "- **Tree-based speculation** (SpecInfer, Sequoia) verifies multiple candidate branches at once.",
        "- Layer-skip and early-exit self-speculation (draft = shallow layers of the same model).",
        "- Supported in vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, TGI, llama.cpp, SGLang, Hugging Face (`assisted_generation`).",
        "",
        "#### When it helps and limits",
        "Helps latency-sensitive, small-batch serving where the GPU is underutilised. At **large batch sizes**, decoding becomes compute-bound and speculation gives less benefit (and wasted verification compute can hurt throughput). Costs: extra memory for the draft model or heads and KV cache, implementation complexity, tuning γ dynamically, and batching mixed acceptance lengths.",
        "",
        "#### Pitfalls",
        "Using a draft with a different tokenizer, mismatched domain (low acceptance), choosing γ too large (wasted verification), evaluating speedups only at batch size 1, and assuming speculative sampling changes output quality (it does not, when implemented correctly)."
      ),
      follow: ["Why is the output distribution identical to the target model's?","What determines the speedup, acceptance rate and draft length?","How do Medusa, EAGLE and n-gram or prompt-lookup drafting differ?"],
      tags: ["llms","speculative decoding","inference","latency"]
    })
